2
00:00:04,207 --> 00:00:06,243
<i>Out here in the middle,</i>

3
00:00:06,277 --> 00:00:09,362
<i>our kids learn early that</i>
<i>a penny saved is a penny earned.</i>

4
00:00:09,397 --> 00:00:11,448
<i>And the more pennies</i>
<i>you save and earn,</i>

5
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:13,166
<i>the more stupid crap</i>
<i>you can buy.</i>

6
00:00:13,217 --> 00:00:16,336
I need $34.95 to buy
a pair of night vision goggles.

7
00:00:16,387 --> 00:00:17,954
Sorry. Wrong family.

8
00:00:17,972 --> 00:00:19,639
Let me see
if this changes your mind.

9
00:00:19,674 --> 00:00:21,007
You could borrow them.

10
00:00:21,058 --> 00:00:22,759
I've seen this place
in the daytime.

11
00:00:22,793 --> 00:00:24,728
I don't need
to see it at night.

12
00:00:24,762 --> 00:00:26,963
What do you need night vision
goggles for, anyway?

13
00:00:26,981 --> 00:00:28,598
I want to read in the dark.

14
00:00:28,632 --> 00:00:30,467
Look, if it's too expensive,
I'm willing to go for the ones.

15
00:00:30,485 --> 00:00:33,687
Without the no-chafe head strap,
but they're clearly not as good.

16
00:00:33,738 --> 00:00:35,305
Brick, the answer's "no."

17
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:36,940
If you want to buy
something dumb you don't need,

18
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:38,492
you gotta be able
to pay for it.

19
00:00:38,526 --> 00:00:39,826
Well, til you get
a credit card.

20
00:00:39,861 --> 00:00:41,778
What happened to
your money from grandma?

21
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:43,330
I spent it on books.

22
00:00:43,364 --> 00:00:44,748
Christmas?
Books.

23
00:00:44,782 --> 00:00:46,366
Birthday?
Books.

24
00:00:46,417 --> 00:00:49,035
Well, that'll teach you to waste
all your money on books. Mm.

25
00:00:49,086 --> 00:00:51,505
Listen, Brick, if you really
want the goggles that bad,

26
00:00:51,539 --> 00:00:53,656
you're gonna have to go out
and earn the money yourself.

27
00:00:53,674 --> 00:00:54,708
Okay.

28
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:56,493
Might I either of you
be interested

29
00:00:56,511 --> 00:00:58,211
in buying some used books?

30
00:01:00,181 --> 00:01:03,049
<i>Meanwhile, Sue was cheering</i>
<i>like there was no tomorrow.</i>

31
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:04,968
<i>It was the last match</i>
<i>of the last meet.</i>

32
00:01:05,002 --> 00:01:07,604
<i>Of the wrestling season,</i>
<i>and everything was on the line.</i>

33
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,841
Aah! We won?

34
00:01:11,859 --> 00:01:13,977
- We won! Aah!
- We're number one!

35
00:01:14,011 --> 00:01:15,678
<i>That's right... </i>
<i>Despite all the odds,</i>

36
00:01:15,696 --> 00:01:19,232
<i>the Orson high wrestling team</i>
<i>had finally won one meet.</i>

37
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:21,401
Matt!
This is the best day ever!

38
00:01:21,452 --> 00:01:23,520
You won the meet,
and I was waiting to tell you,

39
00:01:23,538 --> 00:01:25,739
I'm finally getting
my braces off. Aah!

40
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:28,074
Isn't this exciting?
Why aren't you excited?

41
00:01:28,125 --> 00:01:29,106
I have something to tell you.

42
00:01:29,544 --> 00:01:31,378
Oh, no. What's wrong? Do you
like me better with braces?

43
00:01:31,412 --> 00:01:32,862
I can keep 'em if you want.

44
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:34,531
I really don't mind the swollen
gums and the headaches.

45
00:01:34,549 --> 00:01:35,665
I'm moving.

46
00:01:35,699 --> 00:01:37,033
What?

47
00:01:37,051 --> 00:01:38,134
Where?

48
00:01:38,169 --> 00:01:40,504
To Zionsville.
M-m-my dad got a new job.

49
00:01:40,538 --> 00:01:43,974
No. You... you can't move away.
Everything's perfect now.

50
00:01:44,008 --> 00:01:46,376
You're winning wrestling,
I'm getting my braces off...

51
00:01:46,394 --> 00:01:48,094
We're practically
a power couple.

52
00:01:48,145 --> 00:01:49,813
Look, we'll... we'll e-mail
and call each other,

53
00:01:49,847 --> 00:01:51,348
and it'll be like
nothing changed.

54
00:01:51,382 --> 00:01:53,850
I know it sucks,
but we still have till tomorrow.

55
00:01:53,884 --> 00:01:55,051
<i>Tomorrow?</i>

56
00:01:55,069 --> 00:01:56,553
My dad's already there.

57
00:01:56,604 --> 00:01:57,938
I didn't say anything
'cause I guess I was hoping.

58
00:01:57,989 --> 00:01:59,722
My dad would get fired
or something.

59
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,692
He's not
the most reliable worker.

60
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:08,398
Oh, Matt.

61
00:02:08,416 --> 00:02:10,567
Oh, this must be love,

62
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:15,121
'cause you smell so bad
right now, and I don't care.

63
00:02:17,058 --> 00:02:18,241
I made dinner.

64
00:02:18,259 --> 00:02:20,510
Hey, Axl. You want some fries?

65
00:02:20,545 --> 00:02:23,129
Tater puffs?

66
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,683
- Axl! Are you nuts?
- What's the matter with you?

67
00:02:26,717 --> 00:02:29,586
Coach says
I gotta improve my pickoff move.

68
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,805
If I want to pitch this season.

69
00:02:31,856 --> 00:02:33,807
Ooh!
Stop it.

70
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:36,426
What? Doesn't work if people
see it coming.

71
00:02:37,778 --> 00:02:39,529
Ooh, Sue! Got you corn on the cob.

72
00:02:39,564 --> 00:02:41,414
I know you missed it.

73
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:44,267
This is the worst day ever.

74
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:46,603
Oh, no. What happened?

75
00:02:46,621 --> 00:02:49,706
Well, you know how I had
an overbite-crossbite?

76
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:51,541
Seems they overcorrected,

77
00:02:51,576 --> 00:02:54,110
and now I have
an underbite-overbite.

78
00:02:54,128 --> 00:02:55,445
Is that even a thing?

79
00:02:55,463 --> 00:02:57,947
The doctor said
it's unprecedented.

80
00:02:57,965 --> 00:02:59,883
I'm devastated!

81
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:02,369
This is a catastrophe!

82
00:03:02,403 --> 00:03:04,521
Ow, ow, ow.

83
00:03:04,555 --> 00:03:07,123
I can't even hurl myself!

84
00:03:07,158 --> 00:03:08,892
Oh, honey.

85
00:03:08,926 --> 00:03:11,962
It's not that bad.
I'm sure it's...

86
00:03:11,996 --> 00:03:13,730
How many hours of the day
do you have to wear this?

87
00:03:13,764 --> 00:03:14,948
All of 'em.

88
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:16,816
"All of 'em," huh?

89
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:19,519
Well, you know, you don't
even notice it after a while,

90
00:03:19,553 --> 00:03:23,924
and... and if you wear your hair the right way...

91
00:03:23,958 --> 00:03:25,809
Whoa.
What'd they do to you?

92
00:03:25,843 --> 00:03:28,795
And this isn't
even the worst thing.

93
00:03:28,829 --> 00:03:30,880
Matt's moving away.

94
00:03:30,898 --> 00:03:32,265
Oh, no. Really?

95
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,633
That means he won't
be able to come around.

96
00:03:33,668 --> 00:03:35,902
And hang out here
all the time any more?

97
00:03:36,737 --> 00:03:39,121
What? I'm upset.

98
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:41,257
I'm going to my room.

99
00:03:41,291 --> 00:03:43,843
I think I'd just like to be
alone with my thoughts,

100
00:03:43,877 --> 00:03:46,345
and plus, my lip is bleeding.

101
00:03:46,396 --> 00:03:47,696
Okay.

102
00:03:47,731 --> 00:03:50,132
We'll be in the kitchen
if you need anything.

103
00:03:51,635 --> 00:03:53,786
Ay-yi-yi.

104
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:58,023
Well, I took your advice,
and I got a job.

105
00:03:58,542 --> 00:04:00,493
You're giving Chinese massages?

106
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:02,211
No, underneath that.

107
00:04:02,246 --> 00:04:05,131
You're looking at the new
paperboy at the "Orson herald."

108
00:04:05,165 --> 00:04:11,354
Oh, no. I don't think so, Brick.
You're too young and too...

109
00:04:11,388 --> 00:04:12,888
You know, everything.

110
00:04:12,923 --> 00:04:14,857
But you told me
I should earn my own money.

111
00:04:14,891 --> 00:04:16,542
Yeah, figuring you'd give up.

112
00:04:16,576 --> 00:04:19,712
Look, Brick, a real job
is a lot of responsibility.

113
00:04:19,746 --> 00:04:20,846
I'll approach it with

114
00:04:20,881 --> 00:04:22,999
the same sense of responsibility
I always do.

115
00:04:23,042 --> 00:04:24,833
That's what
we're afraid of. Look...

116
00:04:24,884 --> 00:04:26,251
Hold on, hold on.
You know what?

117
00:04:26,669 --> 00:04:28,119
I think this might
be good for him.

118
00:04:28,137 --> 00:04:29,521
You do?
Mm-hmm.

119
00:04:29,555 --> 00:04:31,957
I'd like to remind you that
we never did find that gerbil.

120
00:04:31,991 --> 00:04:33,458
Brick.

121
00:04:33,492 --> 00:04:36,094
I think what your mom's trying
to say is if we let you do this,

122
00:04:36,129 --> 00:04:37,963
you gotta take it seriously.

123
00:04:37,997 --> 00:04:40,098
There's no quitting,
no "I forgets."

124
00:04:40,132 --> 00:04:43,101
People are depending on you...
Actual people, not just us.

125
00:04:43,636 --> 00:04:45,036
You got it?

126
00:04:45,054 --> 00:04:46,437
You can count on me...

127
00:04:46,471 --> 00:04:49,457
To do whatever it is
we were just talking about.

128
00:04:50,593 --> 00:04:52,343
<i>So Brick was now</i>
<i>a working stiff,</i>

129
00:04:52,361 --> 00:04:53,495
<i>and he attacked his new job.</i>

130
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:56,581
<i>With all the gusto</i>
<i>that he attacked life.</i>

131
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,074
Subtitles downloaded from Podnapisi.NET

132
00:05:15,133 --> 00:05:17,334
Well, Matt and I just talked,

133
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:20,270
and we decided
our love cannot be killed.

134
00:05:20,288 --> 00:05:21,872
Axl!

135
00:05:21,906 --> 00:05:24,925
Anyway,
even though he's moved away,

136
00:05:24,959 --> 00:05:26,143
we're both committed

137
00:05:26,177 --> 00:05:28,262
to maintaining
a long-distance relationship.

138
00:05:28,313 --> 00:05:30,531
It's all very adult
and romantic,

139
00:05:30,565 --> 00:05:32,449
like an Anne Hathaway movie.

140
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:34,268
- Oh, my God.
- Are you hearing this?

141
00:05:34,302 --> 00:05:36,020
Get real, Sue. It's over.

142
00:05:36,054 --> 00:05:39,022
Matt's just the first of many
guys who are gonna "move away."

143
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,858
Axl. Seriously, mom,
tell her she's...

144
00:05:40,892 --> 00:05:42,059
Delusional.

145
00:05:42,093 --> 00:05:43,394
Aah! That's Matt.

146
00:05:43,428 --> 00:05:44,795
Let it ring.

147
00:05:44,813 --> 00:05:46,947
Nancy's looking for volunteers
to pack lunches for the poor.

148
00:05:46,981 --> 00:05:48,799
I do that every morning.

149
00:05:48,833 --> 00:05:50,200
<i>Hello.</i>

150
00:05:50,218 --> 00:05:52,086
<i>Your son or daughter</i>
<i>was absent from school today.</i>

151
00:05:52,137 --> 00:05:53,637
<i>Please have them... </i>

152
00:05:55,106 --> 00:05:57,925
Not me. I mostly went to school today.

153
00:05:57,959 --> 00:05:59,810
I don't skip.

154
00:06:00,344 --> 00:06:04,281
Whew. I'm beat. This whole job
thing is a real grind.

155
00:06:04,315 --> 00:06:07,334
Brick, did you
not go to school today?

156
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:09,185
Yes, I did not.

157
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:11,821
Come on. It took you this long to finish
your paper route?

158
00:06:11,839 --> 00:06:14,591
Oh, I wish. I'd say
I'm roughly two-thirds done.

159
00:06:14,609 --> 00:06:16,392
I just came home
to grab a flashlight.

160
00:06:17,110 --> 00:06:19,695
I really could use those
night vision goggles now, huh?

161
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:21,380
Ironic.

162
00:06:21,415 --> 00:06:23,799
That's out of batteries, like
everything else in this house,

163
00:06:23,817 --> 00:06:25,701
and besides, you're not
going out again. It's too late.

164
00:06:25,736 --> 00:06:27,570
Look, I don't know
what you were doing out there,

165
00:06:27,604 --> 00:06:29,338
but I'll take you out tomorrow.

166
00:06:29,356 --> 00:06:31,574
And see if we can shave
a little time off this route.

167
00:06:32,609 --> 00:06:35,595
You call that a pickoff move?

168
00:06:37,747 --> 00:06:39,181
<i>So the next morning,</i>

169
00:06:39,216 --> 00:06:40,566
<i>Mike took Brick out</i>
<i>to show him how it's done.</i>

170
00:06:40,601 --> 00:06:42,635
Now you can't walk
all the way up to each house.

171
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:44,654
It takes too long. Just throw it
onto the porch from here.

172
00:06:44,688 --> 00:06:47,106
Go ahead. Give it a toss.

173
00:06:48,742 --> 00:06:50,526
Hmm.

174
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:59,869
Really launch it this time,
all the way to the porch.

175
00:07:10,947 --> 00:07:12,263
<i>So once Brick got</i>
<i>the hang of it,</i>

176
00:07:12,281 --> 00:07:14,596
<i>day three of paper delivery</i>
<i>went even better.</i>

177
00:07:14,716 --> 00:07:17,167
Hey, what are you doing?
Shouldn't Brick be doing that?

178
00:07:17,185 --> 00:07:18,269
He's sick.

179
00:07:18,303 --> 00:07:19,487
Really?

180
00:07:19,521 --> 00:07:22,006
Two days on the job,
and he's suddenly sick?

181
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:23,874
No, he is. He threw up.

182
00:07:23,909 --> 00:07:25,876
Ugh. Was it hard to clean up?

183
00:07:25,894 --> 00:07:28,779
You'll let me know.

184
00:07:29,348 --> 00:07:31,099
See? I knew it'd go faster
if we both did it. Mm.

185
00:07:31,133 --> 00:07:33,001
Johnsons... 322.

186
00:07:33,035 --> 00:07:36,404
Yeah. Beats cleaning up barf.
We really should get a dog.

187
00:07:36,438 --> 00:07:38,256
Mm.
Hey, this is weird.

188
00:07:38,307 --> 00:07:40,141
The other day, when I was
putting t-shirts in your drawer,

189
00:07:40,175 --> 00:07:41,592
I found a whole bunch
of batteries.

190
00:07:41,627 --> 00:07:43,428
Yeah.

191
00:07:43,479 --> 00:07:46,787
"Yeah"? Oh, you knew
they were in there?

192
00:07:46,824 --> 00:07:47,528
Yep.

193
00:07:48,034 --> 00:07:49,701
Hey, the Duncans didn't pick up
their paper from yesterday.

194
00:07:49,735 --> 00:07:51,319
So you knew we had batteries?

195
00:07:51,354 --> 00:07:52,287
Mm-hmm.

196
00:07:52,321 --> 00:07:54,094
Okay.

197
00:07:55,446 --> 00:07:56,573
What?

198
00:07:56,901 --> 00:07:58,817
Might have been nice
to say something.

199
00:07:58,845 --> 00:08:00,178
When you knew we needed 'em.

200
00:08:02,098 --> 00:08:04,666
So why didn't you?

201
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:06,168
"Why didn't I" what?

202
00:08:06,802 --> 00:08:08,953
Say something
when we needed batteries.

203
00:08:08,971 --> 00:08:10,621
'Cause I didn't need batteries.

204
00:08:10,639 --> 00:08:12,740
You needed batteries,
and those batteries are mine.

205
00:08:12,775 --> 00:08:14,208
Yours?

206
00:08:14,243 --> 00:08:16,010
Yeah. My batteries.

207
00:08:16,061 --> 00:08:17,762
But we don't just hoard
our own separate batteries.

208
00:08:17,796 --> 00:08:19,630
We have a place for batteries...
The junk drawer.

209
00:08:20,165 --> 00:08:22,132
Yeah, the <i>house</i> batteries
go in the junk drawer.

210
00:08:22,167 --> 00:08:24,585
But there are never any house
batteries <i>in</i> the junk drawer.

211
00:08:24,619 --> 00:08:26,854
Exactly.
I know how this place works.

212
00:08:26,888 --> 00:08:28,205
Open a pack of batteries,

213
00:08:28,239 --> 00:08:31,275
it's like throwing
a loaf of bread to pigeons.

214
00:08:31,309 --> 00:08:34,845
- Great, you missed your throw.
- Now I gotta back up.

215
00:08:37,648 --> 00:08:39,432
Axl, I need your phone.

216
00:08:39,483 --> 00:08:41,918
Sha! Some hot chick
must have got my number.

217
00:08:41,969 --> 00:08:43,719
She's texting me
some pretty serious pg 13 stuff.

218
00:08:43,738 --> 00:08:45,855
No, I need to
tell you something.

219
00:08:45,890 --> 00:08:48,091
Tell me how to spell "fondle"
and then beat it.

220
00:08:48,125 --> 00:08:49,192
Axl, those texts are from Matt.

221
00:08:49,226 --> 00:08:50,627
Right.

222
00:08:50,661 --> 00:08:52,962
Matt's some hot chick who wants
to do stuff to my stuff.

223
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:55,081
No, I used up
all my monthly texts yesterday,

224
00:08:55,116 --> 00:08:57,917
so I told Matt
to text me on your phone.

225
00:08:57,952 --> 00:09:01,087
Oh, God. This <i>is</i> Matt? Uhh!

226
00:09:01,122 --> 00:09:04,257
That is so not cool.
Brrr! Ugh!

227
00:09:04,808 --> 00:09:06,776
And tell him
the picnic by the lake is off.

228
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:09,145
Sorry about that, Matt.
I have the phone now.

229
00:09:09,179 --> 00:09:11,647
Now we can
talk and text at the same time.

230
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,985
You guys have gotta do something
about your daughter.

231
00:09:16,052 --> 00:09:18,020
You two are out
driving around having fun.

232
00:09:18,038 --> 00:09:20,122
While Sue's out there
making a fool of herself.

233
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:22,725
She's going around telling
people that she and Matt.

234
00:09:22,743 --> 00:09:25,895
Are gonna be together forever.
You gotta tell her the truth!

235
00:09:25,962 --> 00:09:27,780
Axl, look.

236
00:09:28,331 --> 00:09:30,682
You guys are getting older,
and it's not our place.

237
00:09:30,717 --> 00:09:33,252
To step in the middle
of all your romantic dramas.

238
00:09:33,286 --> 00:09:34,837
Oh, my God.

239
00:09:34,871 --> 00:09:36,805
That's the laziest
parenting ever.

240
00:09:39,492 --> 00:09:42,244
Mm! Look, mom and dad should be
the ones talking to you,

241
00:09:42,278 --> 00:09:43,712
but they won't
'cause they're lame.

242
00:09:43,746 --> 00:09:45,013
And they know nothing
about relationships.

243
00:09:45,048 --> 00:09:47,749
I mean, if they did, why would
they be with each other?

244
00:09:47,784 --> 00:09:51,053
Axl, I'm sort of busy.

245
00:09:52,722 --> 00:09:55,174
Here's the deal.
You're being a huge dork,

246
00:09:55,208 --> 00:09:57,643
and this isn't even about your
brace face, even though I have,

247
00:09:57,677 --> 00:09:59,311
like, 50 things
I could say on the subject,

248
00:09:59,345 --> 00:10:01,663
but that's for another day.

249
00:10:01,681 --> 00:10:03,779
How can I explain?

250
00:10:04,299 --> 00:10:05,499
Sue, when you started
high school,

251
00:10:05,517 --> 00:10:06,784
you could have stepped it up.

252
00:10:06,819 --> 00:10:08,069
And hung out
with some cooler people.

253
00:10:08,103 --> 00:10:10,471
And <i>maybe</i> had a shot
at a somewhat normal guy,

254
00:10:10,706 --> 00:10:12,657
but what do <i>you</i> do?
You join wrestlerettes.

255
00:10:12,691 --> 00:10:15,192
I founded wrestlerettes.

256
00:10:15,227 --> 00:10:16,327
Making my point for me.

257
00:10:16,378 --> 00:10:17,595
Next, you date a hobbit.

258
00:10:17,629 --> 00:10:19,981
And never once stop to think
how it would affect me.

259
00:10:20,015 --> 00:10:21,532
Before you do anything...

260
00:10:21,566 --> 00:10:23,183
<i>Anything... </i>
You need to ask yourself,

261
00:10:23,201 --> 00:10:25,619
"how does this affect
my super cool brother?"

262
00:10:25,637 --> 00:10:29,323
I'm sorry, Axl, but Matt and I
are gonna be together forever,

263
00:10:29,358 --> 00:10:31,158
so get used to it.

264
00:10:31,193 --> 00:10:32,743
You're nuts.

265
00:10:32,778 --> 00:10:35,029
Long-distance relationships
don't work for guys.

266
00:10:35,063 --> 00:10:36,580
We need our girlfriends
right in front of us,

267
00:10:36,615 --> 00:10:37,915
and even that
doesn't always work.

268
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:39,233
If they're standing next to
their better-looking friend.

269
00:10:39,251 --> 00:10:41,735
Well, Matt isn't
like other guys.

270
00:10:41,753 --> 00:10:44,171
No argument there,
but he's still a guy.

271
00:10:46,274 --> 00:10:49,142
Oh, and, uh, just so you
appreciate how nice I'm being,

272
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:53,514
I haven't said a <i>thing</i>
about your stupid headgear.

273
00:10:53,881 --> 00:10:55,543
And the things
I have are good...

274
00:10:55,663 --> 00:10:58,985
And really funny, but I'm not
saying 'em, not even...

275
00:10:59,003 --> 00:11:01,121
"Cage match," which is
my favorite one I came up with,

276
00:11:01,155 --> 00:11:03,156
so, yeah, you're welcome.

277
00:11:11,363 --> 00:11:12,663
Brick's feeling better.
Should we wake him up?

278
00:11:12,697 --> 00:11:16,250
- Do we have to?
- It's faster without him.

279
00:11:17,652 --> 00:11:18,752
Sue.

280
00:11:18,803 --> 00:11:21,872
- Sue.
- Did you sleep here all night?

281
00:11:22,306 --> 00:11:25,909
Oh. I guess I feel asleep
talking to Matt.

282
00:11:25,943 --> 00:11:28,111
It's tough being the new kid.

283
00:11:28,646 --> 00:11:31,781
Lucky, there's this kind soul
in his homeroom named sheri.

284
00:11:31,815 --> 00:11:33,983
Who's showing him around.

285
00:11:34,018 --> 00:11:35,502
You got a little...

286
00:11:36,036 --> 00:11:38,170
Oh, I gotta decrust.

287
00:11:38,613 --> 00:11:41,141
Oh, I need a butter knife.
It's really in there.

288
00:11:41,261 --> 00:11:43,426
Ow.

289
00:11:45,622 --> 00:11:47,356
By the way...

290
00:11:47,391 --> 00:11:49,258
I know you moved the batteries.

291
00:11:49,293 --> 00:11:51,694
I knew you'd look for 'em,
which is why I moved 'em.

292
00:11:51,729 --> 00:11:54,363
- Fine. Move 'em. I don't care.
- I'll find 'em.  I don't know.

293
00:11:54,398 --> 00:11:56,673
It took you ten years
to find the first ones.

294
00:11:57,124 --> 00:11:58,634
Seriously?

295
00:11:58,668 --> 00:12:01,236
You've had your own batteries
for ten years?

296
00:12:01,254 --> 00:12:03,439
So that time
I couldn't turn my neck

297
00:12:03,473 --> 00:12:05,608
and I needed batteries
for my massager, you had them?

298
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:07,409
Yeah, but I gave you a massage.

299
00:12:07,427 --> 00:12:09,011
Oh, you squeezed
the back of my neck.

300
00:12:09,045 --> 00:12:10,246
And said, "that should do it."

301
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:11,881
Why are you
so upset about this?

302
00:12:11,915 --> 00:12:13,666
'Cause we're married, Mike.
You don't get to have

303
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:16,242
your own secret stash
of bachelor batteries.

304
00:12:16,362 --> 00:12:19,281
Why not? Why can't I have
one thing that's mine?

305
00:12:19,315 --> 00:12:20,398
Because that's not marriage.

306
00:12:20,416 --> 00:12:22,584
When you're married,
you share everything.

307
00:12:22,619 --> 00:12:24,903
Everything?
Yeah.

308
00:12:24,937 --> 00:12:26,805
What about your frosting?

309
00:12:27,381 --> 00:12:29,583
What are you talking about?
That frosting's for anybody.

310
00:12:29,617 --> 00:12:30,984
It's right there
in the cabinet.

311
00:12:31,018 --> 00:12:32,619
Oh, no, I'm not talking
about that frosting.

312
00:12:32,653 --> 00:12:34,788
I'm talking about
the <i>garage</i> frosting.

313
00:12:35,664 --> 00:12:37,065
You know about that?

314
00:12:37,099 --> 00:12:40,351
Few years ago, I came across
a couple cans of frosting.

315
00:12:40,386 --> 00:12:42,570
Hidden behind the paintbrushes,

316
00:12:42,588 --> 00:12:44,439
but did I make
a big deal out of it?

317
00:12:44,473 --> 00:12:47,542
No, 'cause if you want
to have something for yourself,

318
00:12:47,576 --> 00:12:49,293
I'm okay with that.

319
00:12:49,870 --> 00:12:52,605
Yeah, well, you can't possibly
compare frosting to batteries.

320
00:12:52,639 --> 00:12:54,307
They're completely different.

321
00:12:54,341 --> 00:12:55,508
Really? In what way?

322
00:12:55,542 --> 00:12:57,176
Seriously? You don't know?

323
00:12:57,769 --> 00:13:00,304
And why were you snooping around
in the paintbrushes, anyway?

324
00:13:00,338 --> 00:13:03,691
Hmm? You don't even paint.

325
00:13:05,210 --> 00:13:06,627
Sue, what do you think?

326
00:13:06,661 --> 00:13:08,095
Should I upgrade
to the camouflage goggles.

327
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:09,363
For only $5?

328
00:13:09,397 --> 00:13:10,981
Brick, I'm on the phone.

329
00:13:11,016 --> 00:13:14,107
What the heck? I should
treat myself. I'm working hard.

330
00:13:14,227 --> 00:13:15,724
Oh, you have to go?
Okay. Bye.

331
00:13:16,979 --> 00:13:18,813
Sheri's walking Matt to school.
Mm.

332
00:13:18,847 --> 00:13:21,332
I'm so happy he found her.
She is so helpful.

333
00:13:21,366 --> 00:13:24,252
She even showed him
where the movie theater is.

334
00:13:24,286 --> 00:13:26,237
<i>They say love is blind...</i>

335
00:13:26,771 --> 00:13:28,239
Oh, you're studying with Sheri?

336
00:13:28,290 --> 00:13:30,691
Well, thank her for me for
taking such good care of you.

337
00:13:30,725 --> 00:13:33,294
<i>But in Sue's case,</i>
<i>love is also deaf...</i>

338
00:13:33,328 --> 00:13:35,930
It's so great, 'cause Sheri just
broke up with her boyfriend,

339
00:13:35,964 --> 00:13:37,815
so she's kind of lonely, too.

340
00:13:38,366 --> 00:13:41,802
But Matt says she's super funny
and smart and pretty,

341
00:13:41,836 --> 00:13:43,954
so hopefully,
she'll find someone soon.

342
00:13:43,988 --> 00:13:46,790
That way, Matt and I
can double-date.

343
00:13:46,824 --> 00:13:48,792
<i>And kind of dumb.</i>

344
00:13:50,928 --> 00:13:53,396
Here's why frosting's
different from batteries.

345
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:55,982
No one ever needed frosting
in an emergency.

346
00:13:56,050 --> 00:13:57,934
You, last tornado watch.

347
00:13:59,219 --> 00:14:01,387
Why won't you let this go?

348
00:14:01,422 --> 00:14:02,905
So I've got a draw
full of batteries.

349
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:04,807
It's not like
I have a mistress.

350
00:14:05,542 --> 00:14:09,594
A <i>mistress?</i> Wow.
You went there awfully fast.

351
00:14:09,714 --> 00:14:11,632
How did we get
from batteries to mistress?

352
00:14:11,666 --> 00:14:14,502
I'm just saying,
some guys cheat on their wives.

353
00:14:14,536 --> 00:14:16,787
I have batteries.
You should be happy.

354
00:14:16,822 --> 00:14:18,222
Oh, you're right.

355
00:14:18,256 --> 00:14:22,526
Sorry if I don't say it enough,
but thank you for not cheating.

356
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:26,230
- All right.
- This is perfect. Stop the car.

357
00:14:27,748 --> 00:14:29,649
What are you doing?

358
00:14:29,683 --> 00:14:33,269
We have to put the newspapers on
the porches, or they'll get wet.

359
00:14:35,455 --> 00:14:38,107
You should have put the papers
in the plastic things.

360
00:14:38,125 --> 00:14:39,709
Oh, yeah, thanks for that.

361
00:14:39,743 --> 00:14:41,360
How was I supposed to know
it was gonna rain?

362
00:14:41,395 --> 00:14:43,462
It says "rain today"
on top of the paper.

363
00:14:43,497 --> 00:14:46,916
You know what the really sad part is, Mike?

364
00:14:46,950 --> 00:14:48,150
That when we were dating,

365
00:14:48,185 --> 00:14:49,285
you would have shared
your batteries.

366
00:14:49,819 --> 00:14:50,953
Now I gotta stumble across 'em.

367
00:14:50,987 --> 00:14:52,955
While I'm putting away
your giant boxer shorts,

368
00:14:52,989 --> 00:14:55,557
which, by the way, you've also
had since we were dating.

369
00:14:55,575 --> 00:14:56,959
Get back in the car, Frankie.

370
00:14:56,993 --> 00:14:58,594
No! People need
to get their papers.

371
00:14:58,628 --> 00:15:01,263
Yeah, it's, uh, really urgent
they read about...

372
00:15:01,797 --> 00:15:03,431
"95-year-old Jasper Harris,

373
00:15:03,466 --> 00:15:05,333
who died yesterday,
planting tomatoes."

374
00:15:05,367 --> 00:15:06,651
So you just want to quit?

375
00:15:06,686 --> 00:15:08,987
Look, we're trying to
teach our son a lesson here.

376
00:15:09,021 --> 00:15:11,473
It's about showing Brick
how to do a job well.

377
00:15:12,007 --> 00:15:14,125
I'd love to show him!
I'd love to show him right now!

378
00:15:14,159 --> 00:15:15,910
Where the hell is he?!

379
00:15:17,712 --> 00:15:20,280
Morning. I hope you wrapped
the papers in plastic.

380
00:15:20,315 --> 00:15:22,816
Last thing I need is a bunch
of angry phone calls.

381
00:15:22,851 --> 00:15:24,551
That's it, Brick. You quit.

382
00:15:24,586 --> 00:15:26,818
Really? I thought
it was going so well.

383
00:15:26,938 --> 00:15:28,855
I even thought
about adding a second route.

384
00:15:28,873 --> 00:15:30,407
Mom!

385
00:15:30,458 --> 00:15:33,209
Matt just called
and broke up with me.

386
00:15:33,244 --> 00:15:35,962
He says he wants
to see someone else,

387
00:15:35,997 --> 00:15:38,248
and you'll never guess who.

388
00:15:38,282 --> 00:15:39,633
Sheri.

389
00:15:49,284 --> 00:15:51,219
Knock, knock. Hi.

390
00:15:51,253 --> 00:15:54,972
We're just here
to drop off the newspaper bag.

391
00:15:55,007 --> 00:16:00,294
Ohh. You're the folks
whose kid is quitting the route.

392
00:16:00,329 --> 00:16:02,182
Yeah. I'm so sorry.
You got our e-mail?

393
00:16:02,263 --> 00:16:03,144
Oh, sure, sure.

394
00:16:03,163 --> 00:16:04,980
'Course, in my day,
when you quit a job,

395
00:16:04,998 --> 00:16:07,983
you looked a man in the eye
and told him you were quitting.

396
00:16:08,985 --> 00:16:12,004
Uh... okay. We're quitting.

397
00:16:12,038 --> 00:16:14,540
So you're raising a quitter?

398
00:16:14,574 --> 00:16:17,826
Actually, we're raising
three quitters.

399
00:16:17,844 --> 00:16:20,512
That supposed to be a joke?
'Cause in my day,

400
00:16:20,547 --> 00:16:23,215
when you took a job,
it meant something...

401
00:16:23,266 --> 00:16:26,134
A little thing
called "responsibility."

402
00:16:26,168 --> 00:16:28,336
Oh, y... you don't know us,

403
00:16:28,354 --> 00:16:30,222
but we're all about
responsibility.

404
00:16:30,273 --> 00:16:32,173
We take responsibility
very seriously.

405
00:16:32,192 --> 00:16:36,144
And being responsible's why you
let him quit after four days?

406
00:16:36,178 --> 00:16:39,448
Oh, no. He actually quit
way before that.

407
00:16:39,482 --> 00:16:42,317
See, you gotta know Brick.
He starts things he can't...

408
00:16:42,368 --> 00:16:44,319
Well, tell him, Mike.
Tell him what he does.

409
00:16:44,353 --> 00:16:45,988
Mike's the one
who thought he could do it.

410
00:16:46,022 --> 00:16:47,656
I never really thought
he could do it.

411
00:16:47,690 --> 00:16:51,293
And let me guess.
You encouraged him anyway?

412
00:16:51,327 --> 00:16:53,028
Yeah.

413
00:16:53,046 --> 00:16:56,248
You parents today,
always encouraging your kids,

414
00:16:56,299 --> 00:16:58,834
telling 'em what a good job
they're doing.

415
00:16:58,868 --> 00:17:01,169
It's no wonder
they all grow up to be singers.

416
00:17:01,756 --> 00:17:04,592
Look, uh, why don't we just
give you the money back.

417
00:17:04,610 --> 00:17:07,928
And call it a day? What was it,
uh, 8 bucks for the week?

418
00:17:07,946 --> 00:17:10,231
Here's 10. Keep the change.

419
00:17:10,265 --> 00:17:14,035
Oh, sure, just... just throw
your money at the problem.

420
00:17:14,069 --> 00:17:15,787
You rich people make me sick.

421
00:17:15,821 --> 00:17:18,606
No!
Trust me, we're not rich.

422
00:17:18,624 --> 00:17:21,342
Between the two of us,
we have four jobs.

423
00:17:21,376 --> 00:17:22,910
Are you gonna quit those, too?

424
00:17:22,945 --> 00:17:24,412
Hey, now hold on a minute.

425
00:17:24,446 --> 00:17:25,496
No, <i>you</i> hold on.

426
00:17:25,547 --> 00:17:28,132
Okay, let's all hold on.
This is crazy.

427
00:17:28,167 --> 00:17:29,500
We're all getting worked up.

428
00:17:29,551 --> 00:17:31,469
About a silly,
little local newspaper.

429
00:17:31,503 --> 00:17:33,137
"A silly paper."

430
00:17:33,172 --> 00:17:36,757
This "silly, little paper"
put a roof over my head.

431
00:17:36,791 --> 00:17:40,111
Newspapers used to start wars,
take down presidents.

432
00:17:40,145 --> 00:17:43,464
Now nobody seems to have time
to read the paper any more,

433
00:17:43,482 --> 00:17:44,565
too busy running around

434
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:47,602
with their ithings
and their sketch phones.

435
00:17:47,636 --> 00:17:49,821
No, no, I don't have
any of those things.

436
00:17:49,855 --> 00:17:53,107
Jasper Harris died
planting tomatoes.

437
00:17:53,141 --> 00:17:56,644
He was a world war II Vet who
stormed the beaches of normandy,

438
00:17:56,662 --> 00:17:59,864
won the medal of honor,
so you two could have.

439
00:17:59,915 --> 00:18:04,368
The freedom to mollycoddle
your irresponsible kid.

440
00:18:04,419 --> 00:18:07,121
No one's gonna honor Jasper,

441
00:18:07,155 --> 00:18:11,459
'cause CNN isn't coming
to Orson. No, no, no, no.

442
00:18:11,493 --> 00:18:14,328
And you two don't seem
to think it's important.

443
00:18:14,346 --> 00:18:21,969
That this "silly, little local
newspaper" gets delivered.

444
00:18:23,522 --> 00:18:27,108
Eh. Keep your batteries.

445
00:18:27,142 --> 00:18:28,509
<i>Yep, in a family,</i>

446
00:18:28,527 --> 00:18:30,945
<i>sometimes you end up</i>
<i>doing somebody else's job,</i>

447
00:18:30,979 --> 00:18:32,363
<i>but if you're lucky,</i>

448
00:18:32,397 --> 00:18:35,066
<i>someone'll step up</i>
<i>and help you do yours.</i>

449
00:18:37,035 --> 00:18:39,370
Oh, my God. Seriously?

450
00:18:39,404 --> 00:18:42,790
I was upset about Matt,
so I threw myself on the bed,

451
00:18:42,824 --> 00:18:45,042
and then the blanket
got stuck in my headgear,

452
00:18:45,077 --> 00:18:46,794
and now I can't get it out!

453
00:18:46,828 --> 00:18:50,698
I can't believe you're still
moping around over that loser.

454
00:18:50,716 --> 00:18:52,550
Would you hold still?
Ugh.

455
00:18:52,584 --> 00:18:54,919
There. Got it.

456
00:18:56,138 --> 00:18:59,270
But what if nobody ever
loves me like that again?

457
00:18:59,390 --> 00:19:02,093
Ugh. Why do you
not listen to me?

458
00:19:02,144 --> 00:19:05,179
I told you before,
guys could...

459
00:19:05,213 --> 00:19:07,098
Like you.

460
00:19:07,149 --> 00:19:08,321
Really?

461
00:19:08,441 --> 00:19:10,101
Trust me, I see a lot of girls

462
00:19:10,152 --> 00:19:12,069
where I go, "how does
<i>she</i> have a boyfriend?"

463
00:19:12,104 --> 00:19:14,605
But you know what?
They do, so...

464
00:19:14,656 --> 00:19:16,657
Why not you?

465
00:19:16,692 --> 00:19:19,405
Aw, Axl!

466
00:19:19,638 --> 00:19:20,985
<i>If</i> you don't blow it.

467
00:19:21,036 --> 00:19:23,955
Like, sticking your head to
the furniture is not gonna help.

468
00:19:27,776 --> 00:19:29,661
What are you doing right now?

469
00:19:29,712 --> 00:19:32,714
Well, I was gonna
write out the entire lyrics

470
00:19:32,748 --> 00:19:36,084
to "Irreplaceable" by Beyonce
and send it to Matt.

471
00:19:36,118 --> 00:19:38,119
Okay, you're not doing that.

472
00:19:38,137 --> 00:19:39,671
You can come with me
to my baseball game.

473
00:19:39,705 --> 00:19:41,122
I'll revise my previous rule.

474
00:19:41,140 --> 00:19:42,891
About not being
seen with you ever.

475
00:19:42,925 --> 00:19:44,425
To you occasionally
hanging with me.

476
00:19:44,459 --> 00:19:46,911
As long as it's been
cleared in advance.

477
00:19:46,946 --> 00:19:48,930
Thanks, Axl.

478
00:19:48,964 --> 00:19:50,598
Bring $5 for gas and...

479
00:19:50,632 --> 00:19:53,685
change your shoes.
I might need a push start.

480
00:19:57,156 --> 00:20:00,024
Come on, Axl. You can do it.

481
00:20:00,075 --> 00:20:02,327
Go, Axl!

482
00:20:06,815 --> 00:20:10,318
Two outs!
Watch that runner, heck!

483
00:20:17,309 --> 00:20:18,659
Aw!

484
00:20:18,677 --> 00:20:20,962
Sue!

485
00:20:22,298 --> 00:20:25,767
That was a really good
pickoff move, Axl.

486
00:20:25,801 --> 00:20:28,603
I didn't see it coming at all.

487
00:20:31,056 --> 00:20:33,942
Feeling better, honey?

488
00:20:33,976 --> 00:20:36,477
Yeah. The ball deflected
off my headgear,

489
00:20:36,511 --> 00:20:38,029
so it only got my forehead.

490
00:20:38,063 --> 00:20:41,449
How lucky am I
to have headgear, huh?

491
00:20:47,856 --> 00:20:51,192
I don't think these things work
without double "a" batteries.

492
00:20:51,210 --> 00:20:55,630
Do we have double "a" batteries?
Double "a."

493
00:20:55,664 --> 00:20:58,099
I don't know, Mike. Do we?

494
00:20:58,133 --> 00:20:59,801
Nope.

495
00:20:59,835 --> 00:21:01,369
Okay then.

496
00:21:01,387 --> 00:21:04,639
Well, I guess I will go out
to buy some at the store.

497
00:21:04,673 --> 00:21:06,724
Since we don't have any
in the house.

498
00:21:06,759 --> 00:21:08,042
Guess so.

499
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:12,347
All right.
Going out into the cold now.

500
00:21:12,381 --> 00:21:13,848
Bundle up.

501
00:21:13,882 --> 00:21:17,619
Okay then. I'm doing it.

502
00:21:19,071 --> 00:21:21,823
Here I go. I'm off...

503
00:21:21,857 --> 00:21:25,827
To get batteries. Mm-hmm.

504
00:21:27,830 --> 00:21:29,397
Walking down the steps.

505
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:33,071
That night, I went out and
bought a 40-pack of batteries

506
00:21:33,110 --> 00:21:36,312
and hid 'em with my frosting, and
we never fought again...

507
00:21:36,819 --> 00:21:38,181
About that.

508
00:21:38,301 --> 00:21:40,162
Sync and corrections by sp8ky for
www.Addic7ed.Com

509
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,103

